    │   │
    │   ├── physics/               # pure logic, uses lib/math not rl
    │   │   ├── physics_player.zig
    │   │   ├── physics_actor.zig
    │   │   └── force.zig
    │   │
    │   ├── ai/
    │   │   ├── ai.zig
    │   │   ├── aas.zig
    │   │   └── ai_pathing.zig
    │   │
    │   ├── anim/
    │   │   ├── anim.zig
    │   │   └── anim_blend.zig
    │   │
    │   └── script/                # doom's idScript equivalent
    │       ├── script_program.zig
    │       └── script_thread.zig
    │
    ├── scenes/                    # Go's internal/scenes
    │   ├── scene.zig              # Scene interface + Stack
    │   ├── play.zig               # gameplay scene
    │   ├── menu.zig
    │   └── pause.zig
    │
    └── tools/                     # only compiled with -Dtools=true
        ├── radiant/               # map editor
        └── decl/                  # decl browser
@end



Camera is a genuinely tricky placement question because it sits at the boundary between engine and game.

## The problem

The camera *reads* game state (player position, room bounds, shake events) but *writes* to the renderer (view matrix, projection). That makes it feel like it belongs in both places, which means you have to make a deliberate call.

## The rule to decide

**Ask: does the camera contain game rules?**

A camera that just follows a point and passes a matrix to the renderer — **engine**.
A camera that knows about the player, cutscenes, lock-on targets, trauma systems — **game**.

In practice for most games it splits into two pieces.

## Where each piece lives

```
src/
├── engine/
│   └── camera/
│       └── camera.zig        # CameraState — position, target, fov,
│                             # zoom, view + projection matrix output.
│                             # Knows nothing about Player or levels.
│                             # Just math. Calls rl.Camera2D / Camera3D.
│
└── game/
    └── camera.zig            # GameCamera — owns a CameraState,
                              # contains all the rules:
                              #   - follow player with lerp
                              #   - clamp to level bounds
                              #   - trauma/shake system
                              #   - cutscene target switching
                              #   - lock-on logic
```

The dependency arrow is one-way:

```
game/camera.zig
    └── imports engine/camera/camera.zig (CameraState)
    └── imports game/player.zig          (to read position)
    └── imports lib/math                 (lerp, bounds)

engine/camera/camera.zig
    └── imports lib/math only
    └── imports raylib (rl.Camera2D)
```

`engine/camera` never knows `game/` exists — same rule as the rest of the engine.

## What each file actually looks like

```zig
// engine/camera/camera.zig
pub const CameraState = struct {
    position: Vec2,
    target:   Vec2,
    zoom:     f32,
    rotation: f32,

    pub fn toRaylib(self: CameraState) rl.Camera2D { ... }
    pub fn screenToWorld(self: CameraState, p: Vec2) Vec2 { ... }
    pub fn worldToScreen(self: CameraState, p: Vec2) Vec2 { ... }
};
```

```zig
// game/camera.zig
pub const GameCamera = struct {
    state:       CameraState,
    trauma:      f32,          // shake intensity 0-1
    bounds:      ?Rect,        // level clamp bounds

    pub fn follow(self: *GameCamera, target: Vec2, dt: f32) void {
        // lerp state.target toward target
    }

    pub fn addTrauma(self: *GameCamera, amount: f32) void {
        self.trauma = @min(1.0, self.trauma + amount);
    }

    pub fn update(self: *GameCamera, dt: f32) void {
        // apply shake, clamp to bounds, decay trauma
    }

    pub fn toCameraState(self: GameCamera) CameraState {
        // bake shake offset into a final CameraState for the renderer
    }
};
```

Then in `scenes/play.zig`:

```zig
cam.follow(player.position, dt);
cam.update(dt);
renderer.beginFrame(cam.toCameraState());
```

This way `engine/camera` is reusable across any game you make, and `game/camera` is where all the feel and rules live — exactly the same split Doom used for its `idPlayerView` (game) vs. the raw view matrix submitted to `RenderSystem` (engine).
